WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.580
Thanks to Brilliant for supporting this episode
of Journey to the Microcosmos.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:10.019
Go to Brilliant.org/microcosmos to learn how
you can take your STEM skills to the next

00:00:10.019 --> 00:00:20.900
level this year!

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:34.720
Every living thing on this planet has water
in them.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:36.270
Some may have more than others.

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:41.980
And some, like our desiccated tardigrade tuns,
may be able to survive long periods of time

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:43.230
without water.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:47.800
But while those tardigrades may be alive,
they’re not quite living.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:54.960
They’re taking a very protective nap, and
they’ll wake up only when water reappears in their life.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.430
Water’s chemical structure is the basis
of life itself.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:06.110
It shapes the interactions of biological molecules,
maintains the shape and structure of cells,

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:10.610
and drives reactions that power our entire
world.

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:14.920
And so, inside each of us, is an ocean.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:25.890
And that…is why we pee.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:30.890
Getting rid of waste is a complicated, messy
business to untangle, and boiling it all down

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:36.530
to a simple matter of pooping and peeing is
doing a disservice to the variety of organismal

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:38.800
junk around the world.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:44.610
But we are just a humble YouTube channel who
has made one episode about how microbes poop.

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:51.100
And so now, it feels necessary to give equal
due to how they pee.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:57.900
The goal, whether solid or liquid, is the
same: removal.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:01.040
There are things in a body that must come
out.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:04.540
But we cannot just wantonly dump things out.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:08.190
That would be like sweeping any small item
in your house into a trash bag.

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:12.549
Sure, you get rid of some dust, but you’re
also potentially, throwing your wedding ring

00:02:12.549 --> 00:02:13.569
into the dumpster.

00:02:13.569 --> 00:02:21.609
In our bodies, we turn to kidneys to filter
out toxins and ammonia waste while reabsorbing metabolites.

00:02:21.609 --> 00:02:25.150
What doesn’t get reabsorbed gets released
as urine.

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:31.250
But even simpler organisms need to be able
to regulate the water and metabolites inside them.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:37.889
For one, water is a physical thing: it takes
up space and creates pressure inside a cell,

00:02:37.889 --> 00:02:41.499
which is good...up until a point..

00:02:41.499 --> 00:02:48.769
And once you get past that point, organisms
can end up bursting, like this poor microbe here.

00:02:48.769 --> 00:02:53.230
And aside from the terrifying thought that
you could water yourself into bursting, there’s

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:59.200
the matter of maintaining the right balance
of life-sustaining molecules and getting rid of waste.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:05.340
So parameciums and some other single-celled
eukaryotes rely on a set of organelles called

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:11.950
the contractile vacuole complex that are connected
to the outside through a pore.

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:17.230
The contractile vacuoles go through cycles
of filling and expelling.

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:22.849
As the vacuole fills up with fluid from inside
the organism, the pore closes up--keeping

00:03:22.849 --> 00:03:24.749
everything inside the vacuole.

00:03:24.749 --> 00:03:28.209
And when that’s done, the pore opens back
up.

00:03:28.209 --> 00:03:30.540
You know, for the peeing.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:35.959
So there are simple and effective methods
on the one end, and the complex organs on the other.

00:03:35.959 --> 00:03:37.590
So, what comes in the middle?

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:39.970
Well, let’s take a look at this Asplanchna.

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:42.859
It looks weird, even for a rotifer.

00:03:42.859 --> 00:03:48.129
And at around half a millimeter long, it’s
on the large size for what we normally see.

00:03:48.129 --> 00:03:53.389
But it’s still a microscopic metazoan, which
means that unlike our single-celled eukaryote

00:03:53.389 --> 00:03:56.670
friends, its body has organs.

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:01.409
Some of these organs seem roughly familiar,
like the Asplanchna’s stomach.

00:04:01.409 --> 00:04:04.079
And the space around those organs is not empty.

00:04:04.079 --> 00:04:11.590
It’s full of fluid, giving the rotifer something
to monitor and regulate for the sake of its own survival.

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:17.239
To do that, the Asplanchna and other simple
multicellular organisms like it rely on a

00:04:17.239 --> 00:04:23.240
structure called the protonephridium, which
begins--oddly enough—at the end: with a

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:27.180
set of hollow cells called terminal organs.

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:32.759
In Asplanchna or other similar animals like
this Polyarthra, those terminal organs are

00:04:32.759 --> 00:04:41.330
referred to as flame cells because of the
flickering movement inside them created by bundles of cilia.

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:47.830
Some organisms replace those cilia with flagella,
and their terminal organs are called solenocytes instead.

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:54.930
The Asplanchna’s flame cells draw in fluids,
acting as an initial filter that blocks the

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:58.080
larger molecules from passing through.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:02.460
The cells use their cilia to send the fluid
down a coiled tube.

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:08.270
But at that point, there still might be some
important small molecules that the organism

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:12.300
could use in that mixture, like certain sugars
or amino acids.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:18.580
And so as the fluid travels through the tube,
those molecules get selectively reabsorbed

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:20.259
into the rotifer.

00:05:20.259 --> 00:05:25.800
What remains in the protonephridium is the
urine, which gets collected in the bladder,

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:33.939
drained into the cloaca, and then released
into the world.

00:05:33.939 --> 00:05:39.060
A fun sidetone: cloacas act as a sort of multi-purpose
opening.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:42.280
In the case of the rotifer, yes, it is used
for peeing.

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:44.970
It’s also used for giving birth.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:49.800
So maybe you’re swimming in rotifer pee,
or maybe you’re swimming in rotifer babies.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:55.250
Whichever one it is, it all came from the
same place.

00:05:55.250 --> 00:06:00.930
As we mentioned, other organisms have protonephridia
as well, including flatworms.

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:07.870
Some segmented worms, or annelids, also have
a protonephridium, while others have a metanephridium.

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:13.659
The principle is the same: they take in fluid,
and then modify it to reabsorb what’s necessary

00:06:13.659 --> 00:06:16.139
for the organism and remove what isn’t.

00:06:16.139 --> 00:06:22.199
But the metanephridia connect directly to
the worm’s cavity through a ciliated funnel.

00:06:22.199 --> 00:06:27.699
The remarkable thing about waste is that,
like water, we all have to deal with it.

00:06:27.699 --> 00:06:35.599
It is every bit as much a part of life as
the living itself is, proof that our bodies

00:06:35.599 --> 00:06:40.750
have done something, consumed something, made
something.

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:45.129
The only thing more remarkable is that even
when the basic principles of waste management

00:06:45.129 --> 00:06:51.999
stay the same, the end results can look so
different.

00:06:51.999 --> 00:06:58.039
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

